    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190290": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-26 Location: Biomedical Research Building POSITION OBJECTIVE The Lee lab focuses on translational cancer research, with an emphasis on solid tumors like hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) and molecular imaging and targeted radiopharmaceutical therapies. The Research Associate will execute research projects with the aim of developing and evaluating targeted theranostics for cancer imaging and therapy. Under the guidance of the PI, the Research Associate is expected to work independently, design and plan projects, coordinate collaborations, supervise students, manage animal models, and carry out research activities of assigned projects through completion. Before the animal experiments, the Research Associate will utilize mammalian cell culture, molecular cloning, PCR/qRT-PCR, lentiviral transduction, and CRISPR/Cas9-based gene modulation. The Research Associate will keep the PI informed of progress of research projects and provide recommendations on experimental design. The Research Associate will also present data and findings at lab and scientific meetings, seminars, etc., prepare manuscripts, and contribute preliminary data for grant proposals. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS 1. Plan and execute experiments for research projects designed by the principal investigator and may design experiments independently and in collaboration with collaborators. Assist with and coordinate experiments performed by other lab members, ensuring adequate resources, assisting with experimental design and analysis. Oversee progress of the research projects and provide regular progress reports to the PI; and provide input and recommendation to principal investigator regarding significant developments in research projects. Ensure the PI is informed about the results of lab members, and ensure the PI’s experimental strategy is explored by lab members and through independent experiments by the Research Associate. (15%) 2. Conduct experiments to supply scientific information as part of a larger project. Studies involve but are not limited to analysis of mammalian cell culture experiments, DNA/RNA isolation, PCR/qRT-PCR, cloning, lentiviral transduction, and cell-based assays for Cellular uptake, retention and washout of radioligands, as well as cellular toxicity including damages to the organelle levels, as well as preclinical studies using radionuclides for imaging and image-based dosimetry as well as for biodistribution, monitoring for treatment-related toxicity and changes in animal health, and documentation of study outcomes, and therapeutic efficacy evaluation. Maintain accurate records of data and provide findings and recommendations to the PI based on analysis. (30%) 3. Assist the PI in and independently develop new techniques and protocols. Train lab members on new techniques.  (6%) 4. Mentor and train lab staff and students; oversee research work and ensure projects are being completed according to the research plan. This will involve teaching other members of the lab techniques and scientific discovery methods, including students and post-docs. Assist and teach both established and new research techniques to other lab members to facilitate their experiments. Coordinate training for lab members with the EHS and others, as needed. (10%) 5. Present data and findings at laboratory meetings and scientific meetings, seminars, etc. Prepare manuscripts, and contribute preliminary data for grant proposals.  (6%) 6. Independently author research projects. Assemble data and make publication-quality figures from analyzed data. With the highest level of integrity and responsible conduct of research, participate in writing manuscripts as a first author and co-author for discoveries from research performed in the lab, together with the PI and other lab members. (7%) 7. Assess, update, and develop new standard operating procedures (SOPs) for the lab including maintaining radiation safety propofols.  (6%) 8. May supervise research assistants and technicians. Supervise the research and technical work performed by other lab members, ensuring proper lab procedures are followed, and their safety, and ensuring adequate resources are provided for lab work. (6%) 9. Operate laboratory equipment such as tissue-culture and standard molecular biology equipment, including real-time PCR instruments and radiation-detection devices used for quantitative radioactivity measurements with harvested tissues and in preclinical radiopharmaceutical studies. Assist other lab members with general lab maintenance and help oversee and maintain inventory of scientific reagents.  (10%) NONESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS 1. Perform other duties as assigned such as periodic lab surveys. (4%) CONTACTS Department: Frequent contact with PI and lab members, may work with other faculty for collaboration of research projects, contact with administrative staff as needed University: Regular/Occasional contact with other labs for collaboration, Regular/Occasional contact with the Animal Resource Center, Contact with other departments as needed External: Supply vendors, other institutions, funding agencies, etc. as needed Students: Undergraduate and graduate student employees working in the lab SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITY No direct supervisory responsibilities. Will train and provide oversight to lab staff and students. WORKING CONDITIONS Radioisotope research only : This position involves the use of radioactive materials (RAMs) and exposure to ionizing radiation during the preparation and handling of radiopharmaceuticals, radiochemical syntheses, experimental procedures, sample processing, radioactive waste management, and other research activities. All work involving RAMs is conducted under approved institutional Radiation Safety Program policies and Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs), using the principles of ALARA (As Low As Reasonably Achievable) to minimize radiation exposure. Personnel are required to complete institutionally mandated radiation safety training, comply with all applicable institutional and regulatory requirements, and use appropriate personal protective equipment (PPE) and radiation monitoring devices. All RAM-related activities are subject to oversight by the Institutional Radiation Safety Committee (RSC), Environmental Health and Safety (EH&S), and audits and inspections by the Ohio Department of Health (ODH), as well as other applicable federal and state regulatory agencies, including the U.S. Nuclear Regulatory Commission (NRC), where applicable. Animal research only: Case's animal facilities are accredited by the Association for the Assessment and Accreditation of Laboratory Animal Care (AAALAC) and is managed according to the \"Guide for the Care and Use of Laboratory Animals” appropriate Federal Animal Welfare Regulations, and the Public Health Service “Policy on the Humane Care and Use of Laboratory Animals.\"  This position, and all animal research personnel, are subject to internal compliance to SOM Animal Resource Center Standard Operating Procedures and to compliance regulations of the Animal Welfare Act, Public Health Service Policy, AAALAC guidelines, the State of Ohio Veterinary Practice Act, Federal Drug Enforcement Administration regulatory guidelines, US Food and Drug Administration Center for Veterinary Medicine regulations and other applicable regulatory guidelines. QUALIFICATIONS Education/Experience: PhD in Molecular Biology, Pharmacology, Pathology, Biochemistry or similar fields with a focus on DNA damage repair, cancer biology, cell and molecular biology, or biomedical science with at least 10 years of demonstrated laboratory research (both cell and animal works) experience. REQUIRED SKILLS 1. Extensive experience with mammalian cell culture and cell-based experimental methods. 2. Extensive experience with molecular biology techniques including DNA/RNA manipulation, cloning, PCR/qRT-PCR, lentiviral transduction, and CRISPR/Cas9-based gene modulation, as well as experience with radiopharmaceutical-based research using various radionuclides for imaging and treatment studies. 3. Excellent analytical skills; ability to formulate findings and recommendations from the analysis. 4. Excellent oral and written communication skills and interpersonal skills; must demonstrate the ability to effectively and professionally communicate and work with various individuals within and external to the University. 5. Strong organization skills; ability to multi-task, prioritize, and meet deadlines.  Must demonstrate attention to detail and accuracy, time management skills, and follow-through. Must be able to work under pressure and conform to shifting priorities, demands, and timeline. 6. Effective leadership skills; ability to lead, train, work with, and elicit cooperation from team members and staff. 7. Ability to work effectively independently and collaboratively within a team.  Must be highly motivated, responsible, dependable, and a self-starter. 8. Effective problem-solving skills; must demonstrate innovation and creativity, sound judgment, and good decision-making. 9. Good computer skills, including database management. Ability to learn new programs as needed. Proficiency with Microsoft Office, GraphPad Prism, and ImageJ; familiarity with Adobe Illustrator. 10. Animal research only: Must demonstrate compliance with CWRU animal research and care (ARC) policies and procedures and compliance to regulations of the Animal Welfare Act, Public Health Service Policy, AAALAC guidelines and other applicable regulatory guidelines. 11. Animal research only: Must demonstrate compassion for animals within CWRU facilities and dedication to the Animal Resource Center’s mission.  Must handle animals with care and respect at all times. 12. Animal Research only: Must be able and willing to maintain a high standard of personal cleanliness and utilize protective gear to protect the health of the animals. Case Western Reserve University is committed to providing a transparent estimate of the salary for this position at the time of its posting. The starting annual salary is $35,000-$35,500, depending on qualifications, experience, department budgets, and industry data. (This is a part time 20 hour per week position. 0.5 FTE). Employees receive more than just a paycheck. University employees enjoy a comprehensive benefits package that includes excellent healthcare, retirement plans, tuition assistance, paid time off, and a winter recess.",
      "department": "Radiology - Staff"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/175891": {
      "title": "Assistant Dean of Phd",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-02-19 Location: Graduate Education Office The Assistant Dean for PhD Programs in the School of Medicine (SOM) will provide innovative and dynamic leadership for the school’s PhD programs and related activities. The Assistant Dean reports directly to the Vice Dean (VD) for Graduate Education to fulfil the vision and strategic management for SOM PhD programs. The Assistant Dean will work together with the VD, the Graduate Education Office (GEO) Director of Finance and Operations, GEO staff, Director of Career and Professional Development, the PhD program directors, department chairs, and the BSTP and MSTP Directors to ensure that best practices in PhD training are upheld by all programs, and help develop new initiatives that add value for current and future SOM PhD programming and help ensure the success of our students. Additionally, the Assistant Dean will work closely with the Enrollment Management Office and University Marketing and Communications to help recruit highly qualified students nationally to SOM programs. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS 1. Program Management ● Meet monthly with PhD program directors to create a dialogue about their programmatic needs, identify strategic initiatives, and maintain bi-directional communication regarding policies and procedures affecting PhD students. ● Guide and assist PhD program directors in managing and resolving administrative hurdles and student-related issues. Manage mentor/student separations. ● With the Office of Finance, develop and enforce policies to ensure prospective PhD mentors from all programs meet specified requirements. ● Develop and implement consistency in mentor/student compacts and financial agreements across all PhD Programs. ● Work with the VD, Vice Provost, Dean of Graduate Studies, and GEO staff to develop a robust mentor training program for PhD mentors, and track compliance. ● Oversee the development of annual calendar and assist VD and administration with program reviews for State approvals and accreditations. ● Work with the Medical Education and Research Evaluation (MERE) team and program directors to develop surveys that help capture and report data about alumni outcomes and satisfaction with their education. Analyze survey and outcomes data to develop continuous quality improvement of educational and value-added programming. ● Work closely with the Director of finance and Operations to ensure uniformity and consistency in operations and finance verifications during student placement across all programs 2. Program Development ● Working with the VD, facilitate the development of new PhD programs to fulfill unmet national needs for our students and help guide these new initiatives through institutional and State of Ohio approval processes. ● Collaborate with the Director of Career and Professional Development to create and manage activities that add-value to SOM PhD program curricula. 3. Recruiting, Admissions, and Student Success ● Work with the Enrollment Management Office, PhD program directors, and University and SOM Marketing and communications to develop and implement strategies to enhance the national recruitment of high caliber prospective students for all PhD Programs. Engage students, alumni, faculty, and staff in recruitment activities. ● Working with the VD and the Assistant Dean for Master’s Programs, develop and serve on the Committee on Students to manage incoming reports from the AccountabilityManagement System (AMS). The annual starting base salary range for this position is from   $15,000- $25,000. This is a part time position and will cover 10% of institutional salary and an additional $7500 annual salary. CWRU considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. University employees enjoy a comprehensive benefits package that includes excellent healthcare, retirement plans, tuition assistance, and paid time off. Full time faculty position at Case Western Reserve University, School of Medicine Please upload a CV and letter of interest",
      "department": "Medical Education (GMS) (FAC/RA/RS)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/175900": {
      "title": "Assistant Dean of Masters Programs",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-02-19 Location: Graduate Education Office The Assistant Dean for Master’s Programs in the School of Medicine (SOM) will provide innovative and dynamic leadership for the school’s 18 Graduate Education master’s programs and current and future certificate programs. The Assistant Dean reports directly to the Vice Dean (VD) for Graduate Education to fulfil the vision and strategic management for SOM master’s and certificate programs and related activities. The Assistant Dean will work together with the VD, the Graduate Education Office (GEO) Director of Finance and Operations, GEO staff, Director of Career and Professional Development, master’s program directors, and department chairs to ensure that best practices in master’s training are upheld by all programs, to develop and create new initiatives that add value for current and future SOM master’s and certificate programming, and to help ensure the success of our students Major activities include collaborating with other SOM and University offices to help improve the recruitment, advising, admissions, matriculation, student success, academic planning, program reviews and assessments, and advancing the overall quality of our existing master’s and certificate programs. The AD will help evaluate the SOM’s portfolio of master’s and certificate programming and strategically direct improvements to existing programs and the development of new programming. Additionally, the Assistant Dean will work closely with the Enrollment Management Office and University Marketing and Communications to help recruit highly qualified students nationally to SOM programs. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS 1. Program Management ● Work with department chairs, master’s program directors, and faculty to strategically plan innovative, novel initiatives to strengthen current master’s and certificate programs. This includes the strategic evaluation and continual improvement of each program to ensure alignment with institutional goals, workforce needs, and national trends. ● Review and update GEO academic policies and procedures affecting master’s students, with engagement from the VD and the School of Graduate Studies. ● Meet monthly with master’s program directors to create a dialogue about their programmatic needs, identify strategic initiatives, and maintain bi-directional communication regarding policies and procedures affecting master’s students. ● Guide and assist master’s program directors and the School of Graduate Studies in managing and resolving administrative hurdles and student-related issues. ● Assist the VD and GEO staff in performing program reviews for state approvals and accreditations. ● Work closely with GEO Director of Operations and Finance to ensure uniformity and consistency in operations and finance across all programs. 2. Program Development ● Facilitate the development of new master’s and certificate programs to fulfill unmet regional and national needs for our students and help guide these new initiatives through institutional and State of Ohio approval processes. This responsibility involves working with potential program directors in the strategic creation and direction of new potential programs from conception to implementation, including market analysis, feasibility studies, and implementation of the new program, if warranted. ● Collaborate with the Director of Career and Professional Development to create and manage activities that add-value to master’s and certificate program curricula. ● Develop opportunities for cross-programmatic engagement and community building activities for master’s students. ● Identify and develop individualized opportunities for students with interests in inter- school and intra-school collaborations that fulfil each student’s educational needs. ● Develop the PRIME + program/track and additional co-curricular activities that aim to enhance postbaccalaureate and master’s students’ experiences as they prepare to apply to medical school or other professional programs. 3. Recruiting, Admissions, and Student Success ● Work with the Enrollment Management Office and master’s program directors to develop and implement strategies to enhance national recruitment and matriculation of high caliber prospective students for all master’s and certificate programs. Engage students, alumni, faculty, and staff in recruitment activities. ● Represent SOM programs at recruiting events locally and nationally, such as Graduate School fairs and with pre-health advisors. Engage students, alumni, faculty, and staff in recruitment activities. ● Serve as a concierge to help guide prospective students to the appropriate SOM programs that help them achieve their educational goals. ● Work with the program directors and the GEO student success specialist to help identify, support, and manage struggling and problematic students ● Working with the VD and the Assistant Dean for PhD Programs, develop and serve on a Committee on Students to manage incoming reports from the Accountability Management System (AMS). 4. Program Support and Data Management ● Work with the Enrollment Management Office and the Office of Finance and Planning to establish and communicate annual enrollment goals and tuition revenue targets to programs. Proactively track progress with the Enrollment Management Office throughout the recruitment cycle and regularly report forecasted results to each program and the office of Finance and Planning. ● Ensure that program director is capturing and reporting data in a comparable manner between programs regarding applications, matriculation, metrics of progression through the degree-granting programs. ● Work with the Medical Education and Research Evaluation (MERE) team and program directors to develop surveys that help capture and report data about alumni outcomes and satisfaction with their education. ● Analyze survey and outcomes data to develop continuous quality improvement of educational and value-added programming, ensuring that each program’s efficacy and strategic direction are aligned with institutional and student success metrics. The annual starting base salary range for this position is from $36,000 - $60,000. This is a part ime position with 30% salary coverage and and additional $7,500 annually. CWRU considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. University employees enjoy a comprehensive benefits package that includes excellent healthcare, retirement plans, tuition assistance, and paid time off. Experience: A record of successfully building or expanding graduate education programs, with preference for those engaged with Master’s programming. Applicants must currently hold a faculty appointment in the SOM and should possess a record of outstanding administrative leadership and managerial experience. A demonstrated history of successful mentorship (at various educational levels) and collegiality and collaboration with faculty, students, staff and administrators. A record of successful fiscal management. Education/Licensing: MD, PhD, or EdD required. Please upload CV and letter of interest",
      "department": "Medical Education (GMS) (FAC/RA/RS)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/185379": {
      "title": "Assistant Dean for Longitudinal Clinical Education",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-06-15 Location: Cleveland, OH This position reports directly to the Associate Dean for Curriculum and serves as the primary leader for the four-year arc of clinical education for the WR2/UP medical students. Essential Functions: Oversee the entire arc of clinical education from matriculation to graduation. This includes oversight and contribution to Block 8: Foundations of Clinical Medicine and all its components (FCM Seminars, Clinical Skills Curriculum, Communications Curriculum, Clinical Reasoning) Manage and mentor the Block 8 FCM leaders, including the FCM Seminars co-leaders, the FCM Director of Communications Curriculum, and the FCM Director of Clinical Skills Curriculum. Collaborate with the Director of Assessment and oversee the clinical component of the third-year Clinical Skills Exam (CSE3) for all CWRU SOM students (WR2/UP and LCP). Coordinate clinical education enhancements, including BLS and ACLS certifications for medical students (M2 and M4) and the Transition to Residency Program (M4). Serve as a design team member with special focus on integration of clinical skills, including clinical reasoning across the curriculum to enhance preclerkship content, the clinical clerkships, and clinical electives Periodically attend relevant class sessions (e.g., FCM seminars, Communication Workshops, Physical Diagnosis sessions, SAMI, etc.) for purposes of session evaluation, improvement and curricular integration. Provide input to the Office of Assessment on the generation and improvement of formative and summative assessments for clinical skills of medical students. Provide oversight of student remediation efforts for clinical skills. Attend weekly Assistant Dean meetings; monthly Joint Clinical Oversight Group, Western Reserve2 Curriculum Committee, Committee on Medical Education; periodic Program Evaluation Committee and Continuous Quality Improvement; annual Medical Education Retreat, and annual Block Leaders and Clerkship Directors Retreats. Present an annual evaluation of the longitudinal clinical education programs with an action plan for improvement for the following year to the WR2 Curriculum Committee. Work closely with the Associate Dean for Curriculum to document clinical skills and communications data for LCME Accreditation efforts. Required Skills: The Assistant Dean for Longitudinal Clinical Education should possess exceptional leadership and management skills, including: Advanced leadership capabilities with demonstrated success in leading large-scale organizational initiatives Exceptional communication skills with the ability to articulate complex ideas clearly to diverse audiences and tailor messages appropriately Consensus-building expertise with proven ability to navigate competing priorities and build agreement among stakeholders with varied perspectives High degree of emotional intelligence and interpersonal skills to build trust, manage conflict, and motivate others Data-driven decision-making with the ability to collect, analyze, and use data to inform curriculum renewal strategies Deep knowledge of medical education, including curriculum design principles, pedagogical methods, assessment strategies, and accreditation standards Understanding of educational innovation, including emerging technologies, AI applications in education, and evidence-based teaching practices Cultural competence and commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion in curriculum design and implementation Flexibility and adaptability with a willingness to adjust plans based on feedback and changing circumstances Collaborative mindset with the ability to work effectively across organizational boundaries and build strong partnerships Support This position will provide 0.3 FTE support from the Office of Medical Education. Administrative support for the Assistant Dean includes: The resources of the Office of Curricular Affairs Office space in the Health Education Campus Travel funds: $3,000/academic year to support travel to professional meetings relevant to your educational role Performance Evaluation Prior to the annual performance evaluation, you will submit a self-assessment to the Vice Dean of Medical Education summarizing your performance in your role as Assistant Dean. At a minimum, this will include documentation of: Effective leadership for pre-clerkship and longitudinal clinical skills training across the 4-year medical education program. Curriculum program improvements and outcomes Evidence of scholarship and dissemination of educational innovations, either by you or those you mentor (faculty members or students). The annual starting base salary range for this position is from $70-120,000. CWRU considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. University employees enjoy a comprehensive benefits package that includes excellent healthcare, retirement plans, tuition assistance, and paid time off. Extensive experience working with pre-clerkship and clerkship medical students in the development of clinical skills. Knowledge and experience working in the WR2 Curriculum is preferred. MD or DO degree is required. Eligibility for faculty appointment at the Assistant Professor level or higher at Case Western Reserve University School of Medicine Minimum of five years of experience in medical education with demonstrated progressive leadership responsibility Significant experience with curriculum design, implementation, and student assessment strategies in medical education Strong understanding of LCME accreditation standards and requirements Preference for individuals with evidence of scholarship in medical education, i.e., peer-reviewed publications and presentations Interested candidates should apply through Interfolio by submitting a CV, cover letter, and the names of three references.",
      "department": "Medical Education (GMS) (FAC/RA/RS)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/186687": {
      "title": "Director for WR2 MD Program Renewal",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-20 Location: Cleveland, OH This position reports directly to the Vice Dean for Medical Education and serves as the primary coordinator for a comprehensive medical education program renewal initiative within the WR2 University Program. The Director for WR2 MD Program Renewal will lead the strategic planning, implementation, and evaluation of program transformation, with particular emphasis on integrating artificial intelligence into the curriculum, creating robust early longitudinal clinical experiences, expanding innovative pedagogical approaches that promote engaged and reflective learning, supporting competency-based medical education, consideration of an accelerated educational track, and ensuring seamless coordination across all phases of medical education. This role requires change leadership expertise, including exceptional organizational and management skills, with a strong ability to build consensus among diverse stakeholders. The  Director will work collaboratively with the Associate Dean for Curriculum, the Associate Dean for Accreditation, CQI, and Educational Analytics, the Associate Dean for Program Innovation and Educational Enhancements, the Associate Dean for Student Affairs, the Assistant Deans for Basic Science, Longitudinal Clinical Education, and Clinical/Clerkship Education, the Executive Director of Curricular Affairs, and faculty leadership across departments to ensure successful  MD program renewal that maintains LCME accreditation standards while advancing educational innovation. As a key member of the medical education leadership team, this position will support the School of Medicine's Strategic Plan and work closely with the Vice Dean for Medical Education to maximize resource utilization and ensure alignment with institutional priorities. Essential Functions: Leadership of WR2 MD Program Renewal Initiative: Serve as the primary project leader for all program renewal activities, developing comprehensive implementation timelines, milestones, and accountability structures Coordinate program renewal working groups and task forces, ensuring representation from all relevant stakeholders including faculty, students, clinical partners, and administrative leadership Facilitate regular communication about program renewal progress to all stakeholders through multiple channels, including participation in relevant curricular committees Lead the integration of artificial intelligence tools and technologies into the curriculum, working with faculty to identify appropriate applications and implementation strategies Champion discovery-based pedagogy, active learning, team-based learning, reflective practice, and the appropriate use of simulation and technology-enhanced education to optimize student learning Build and maintain strong relationships with teaching faculty across the four-year curriculum and mentor individual faculty leaders to build consensus and ensure support for curriculum changes Facilitate town halls, focus groups, and feedback sessions with students, faculty, and staff to gather input, address concerns, and share updates Create communication materials and presentations to articulate the vision, rationale, and benefits of program renewal Identify faculty development needs related to program renewal and coordinate with the Vice Dean for Medical Education to design and deliver appropriate training programs Support faculty in adapting teaching methods and content to align with renewed curriculum goals; Recognize and celebrate faculty contributions to program renewal efforts Curriculum Design and Implementation: Work with the Associate Dean for Curriculum and Assistant Deans to redesign curricular structure, sequencing, and integration across all phases of medical education; Ensure horizontal and vertical integration of content, eliminating redundancies and identifying gaps Coordinate the development of new courses, blocks, or modules as required by the renewed curriculum while aligning the curriculum with a competency framework and ensure mapping to program objectives Collaborate with the Office of Student Assessment to design assessment strategies that align with renewed curriculum goals; Ensure that assessment methods support student learning and provide meaningful feedback Ensure all program renewal activities maintain compliance with LCME accreditation standards: Coordinate with the Associate Dean for Curriculum and the Associate Dean for Accreditation on documentation and reporting details of updated curriculum Support communication strategy with LCME related to curriculum changes Collaboration and Coordination: Work closely with all stakeholders, including the Associate Dean for Curricular Affairs at the Lerner College Program to ensure alignment of curriculum renewal Partner with the Executive Director of Curricular Affairs on operational and logistical aspects of curriculum implementation Document efforts to coordinate with the Lerner College Program to ensure alignment and comparability between the two curricular programs Build relationships with clinical education sites to support implementation of program renewal in clinical settings; Engage with community partners, patients, and other stakeholders as appropriate to support program renewal goals Represent the School of Medicine at regional and national meetings related to curriculum innovation; Seek opportunities for collaboration and shared learning with other medical schools undertaking curriculum renewal Resource Management and Sustainability: Identify opportunities for external funding to support program renewal activities Explore cost-effective strategies for implementing curriculum changes Ensure efficient use of faculty time, physical space, and educational technologies Maintain comprehensive documentation of program renewal processes, decisions, and outcomes Prepare regular progress reports for the Associate Dean for Curriculum and Vice Dean for Medical Education; Contribute to annual curriculum reports and accreditation documentation Required Skills: The  Director for WR2 MD Program Renewal should possess exceptional leadership and project management skills, including: Advanced change leadership capabilities with demonstrated success in leading large-scale organizational transformation initiatives Expert project management skills including the ability to manage multiple complex projects simultaneously, develop realistic timelines, and ensure accountability Strategic thinking and planning abilities to anticipate challenges, identify opportunities, and develop comprehensive implementation strategies Exceptional communication skills with the ability to articulate complex ideas clearly to diverse audiences and tailor messages appropriately Consensus-building expertise with proven ability to navigate competing priorities and build agreement among stakeholders with varied perspectives High degree of emotional intelligence and interpersonal skills to build trust, manage conflict, and motivate others Data-driven decision-making with ability to collect, analyze, and use data to inform curriculum renewal strategies Deep knowledge of medical education including curriculum design principles, pedagogical methods, assessment strategies, and accreditation standards Understanding of educational innovation including emerging technologies, AI applications in education, and evidence-based teaching practices Flexibility and adaptability with willingness to adjust plans based on feedback and changing circumstances Collaborative mindset with ability to work effectively across organizational boundaries and build strong partnerships Working Conditions: The Director for WR2 MD Program Renewal will be expected to work regularly in the medical school environment at the Health Education Campus and engage with faculty, students, and leadership in both the WR2 University Program and the Lerner College Program. This position requires flexibility in scheduling, as program renewal activities may require meetings, workshops, and events outside of traditional business hours. Some travel may be required for site visits to clinical education locations, professional development, or representation at national meetings. This position will provide at a minimum 0.4 FTE support from the Office of the Vice Dean for Medical Education, with additional FTE support negotiable based on candidate qualifications and departmental affiliation. Administrative support will be provided. Term: This position is a term appointment for 4 years, with an option for renewal. The annual starting base salary range for this position is from $80-120,000. CWRU considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. University employees enjoy a comprehensive benefits package that includes excellent healthcare, retirement plans, tuition assistance, and paid time off. Required: PhD, MD, EdD, or equivalent terminal degree Eligibility for faculty appointment at the Assistant Professor level or higher at Case Western Reserve University School of Medicine Minimum of five years of experience in medical education with demonstrated progressive leadership responsibility Significant experience with curriculum design, implementation, and evaluation in medical education Proven track record of successfully leading complex change initiatives or major projects Strong understanding of LCME accreditation standards and requirements Preferred: Experience with curriculum renewal or transformation initiatives in medical education Familiarity with competency-based medical education and entrustable professional activities (EPAs) Knowledge of artificial intelligence applications in medical education Experience with integrated curriculum models Scholarship in medical education as evidenced by peer-reviewed publications and presentations Interested candidates should apply through Interfolio by submitting a CV, cover letter, and the names of three references.",
      "department": "Medical Education (GMS) (FAC/RA/RS)"
