    "https://apply.interfolio.com/166217": {
      "title": "Admissions Postdoctoral Scholar",
      "description": "Posted: 2025-07-25 Location: School of Law As a Postdoctoral Scholar, you will be engaged in research projects funded by grants obtained by others at the University or funded by other department funds. The primary purpose of which is to provide research and/or scholarly training for a career in academia and/or in research. Under the direction of Kelli Curtis, JD and Alyson Alber, JD in the School of Law at CWRU, you will be engaged in research activities that will include, but may not be limited to the following: The postdoc will represent the School of Law at approximately 40 Admissions Recruiting Events around the country; they will serve as a primary file reader on applications to our Fall 26 in person and Fall 26 online JD programs; they will meet with prospective students visiting the School of Law; They will assist the Associate Deans with strategic review of Admissions programs and policies, support the faculty admissions committee, and review federal regulations related to the Budget Reconciliation Act of 2025 to inform enrollment and financial aid policy at the law school and dual degree programs within the University. JD is required.  Admissions experience preferred.",
      "department": "School of Law (PD)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/182288": {
      "title": "Web Content Editor, Junior Programmer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-04-24 Location: On campus POSITION OBJECTIVE Under moderate supervision, perform a variety of tasks using UTech’s tools supporting Case School of Engineering. Using the department's CMS system the candidate will be responsible for adding web content on Drupal 10 websites. Willing to learn the process of creating a new site on the system, manage users and be able to do training as needed for other content editors. Great opportunity to work with other senior Software Developers, learn and gain experience while still in school. Hourly Rate : 13.00-15.00/hour based on experience. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS 1. Create Content on Drupal sites 2. Create views, lists of content types 3. Schedule and perform trainings for other content creators 4. Perform other tasks as assigned by manager 5.  Strong communication and written skills Other SKILLS 1. Good knowledge of Microsoft Products. Microsoft Office. 2. Good knowledge of Windows Operating system. 3. Knowledge of Content Management systems like Drupal and Wordpress 4. Knowledge of front end development languages such as html and css 5. Knowledge of programming languages. Preferred php, python, javascript The School of Engineering Web department will provide training for all new hired student employees. 1. Good knowledge of Microsoft Products. Microsoft Office. 2. Good knowledge of Windows Operating system. 3. Knowledge of Content Management systems like Drupal and Wordpress 4. Knowledge of front end development languages such as html and css 5. Strong communication and written skills Aply through Interfolio",
      "department": "Case School of Engineering (STUDENT)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188934": {
      "title": "Student Assistant – Microsoft 365 Builder & Support",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-06 Location: Case School of Engineering Position Summary We’re looking for a student who can help build tools, templates, and workflows across Microsoft 365. The work supports the Case School of Engineering's operations and includes creating SharePoint pages, Microsoft Lists, Power Automate flows, and basic Power BI visuals. This is a good fit for someone who likes solving problems, learning new technology, and organizing information. ⸻ Key Responsibilities Build and update SharePoint sites, pages, lists, and document libraries. Create and maintain Microsoft Lists using calculated columns, lookups, views, and JSON formatting Help design, test, and refine Power Automate workflows. Build and format HTML-based email templates for automated notifications. Prepare small Power BI visuals or dashboards when needed. Document build steps, organize project materials, and keep notes current Troubleshoot issues related to lists, permissions, flows, and connected data. Support other Microsoft 365 projects or improvements as they come up. ⸻ Work Expectations •    10-20 hours per week on average. •    Primarily on site. •    Must be reliable, responsive, and comfortable collaborating with staff. •    Ability to balance priorities and meet timelines. ⸻ Who Thrives in This Role •    Students who enjoy building and refining tools in Microsoft 365 Power Platform. •    People who like clear goals, hands-on problem solving, and learning new systems. •    Students who want real-world experience with tools used in higher-ed and corporate settings. Hourly rate will vary depending on experience, between $17 - $19.50/hour Required Skills •    Strong working knowledge of Microsoft 365, especially SharePoint, Lists, Excel, Power Automate, and Power BI. •    Ability to write or edit HTML for email templates and formatting. •    Ability to write or edit JSON formatting for Lists and SharePoint (or learn it quickly). •    Strong Excel skills, including formulas and data validation. •    Comfortable learning new tools and working independently. •    Strong attention to detail and accuracy. •    Clear communication skills and ability to explain steps in simple terms. Preferred Skills •    Familiarity with SharePoint or Microsoft 365 permission structures. •    Experience building dashboards, templates, or trackers. •    Interest in process improvement and organizing information. ⸻ Upload resume and brief cover letter indicating why you are interested in this role.",
      "department": "Case School of Engineering (STUDENT)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189543": {
      "title": "CO-OP Student Polymer Research – Vitrimerization & Sustainable Materials",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-19 Location: On-Site (Kent Hale Smith) Transforming Thermoset Waste into Reprocessable Materials Thermosets are widely used in automotive components, electronics, adhesives, and composites. However, their permanently crosslinked networks prevent melting and reprocessing, making recycling challenging. Our group is developing vitrimerization , an innovative technology that transforms crosslinked thermosets into reprocessable materials with dynamic, exchangeable networks. These materials retain the stability and mechanical properties of conventional thermosets while enabling reshaping and reprocessing at elevated temperatures. Our research spans multiple polymer systems and includes academic and industry-supported projects. We are seeking motivated co-op students to contribute to research in polymer recycling, vitrimerization, sustainable materials, and polymer processing. Students will receive hands-on training and work with graduate students and researchers. Responsibilities include polymer sample preparation and processing, laboratory equipment operation, materials characterization, data analysis, literature review, and experimental documentation. Students will gain experience with polymer processing and mechanical, thermal, rheological, and spectroscopic characterization while developing skills in experimental design, troubleshooting, data analysis, and technical communication. Opportunities may also be available to contribute to publications and patent applications. The salary for this position is $20/hour. We are looking for students who: • Are enrolled in Polymer Science and Engineering, Chemical Engineering, Materials Science and Engineering, Chemistry, Mechanical Engineering, or a related field. • Have a strong academic record and interest in experimental research and sustainable materials. • Are detail-oriented, reliable, organized, and able to document experimental work carefully. • Are willing to learn new laboratory techniques and equipment. • Can work effectively independently and as part of a research team. Previous polymer research experience is helpful but not required. Training will be provided. Commitment Applicants must be able to commit to the position for at least one year . Subject: Co-op Application Applicants may briefly describe their academic background, research interests, relevant experience, and availability.",
      "department": "Case School of Engineering (STUDENT)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/166807": {
      "title": "Graduate Research Assistant - Statistical Analysis (Student)",
      "description": "Posted: 2025-08-11 Location: 9501 Euclid Ave Suite 359, Cleveland OH 44106 The Marian K. Shaughnessy Nurse Leadership Academy at the Frances Payne Bolton School of Nursing, Case Western Reserve University, is seeking graduate students currently pursuing a Master of Science in Statistics to assist with data analysis and research support for nurses in Hong Kong. These nurses are working on evidence-based or research-focused projects, and the selected graduate students will play a crucial role in analyzing and interpreting the data they have collected. Key Responsibilities: Work directly with nurses in Hong Kong on various evidence-based or research-focused projects. Assist in cleaning and checking data to ensure accuracy and integrity. Help nurses define variables of interest for their research. Conduct quantitative analysis for each assigned project, including descriptive statistics (means, standard deviations, frequencies), t-tests, Mann-Whitney U-test, Pearson correlation coefficients, and multiple regressions. Perform qualitative analysis, as needed for each assigned project, including content analysis and coding using NVivo15 software. Assist with the interpretation of project results and findings. Provide remote support, coordinating work around your own schedule. Job Details: Remote work with flexible scheduling options. Each project is anticipated to require 4-5 hours of work maximum. Opportunity to gain experience in healthcare research and international collaboration. Must be enrolled in the Master of Science or PhD in Statistics program at Case Western Reserve University. Strong proficiency in statistical analysis and quantitative methods is required as this will be the majority of the work. Experience with SPSS is required. Experience with R and RStudio for statistical analysis is preferred but not required. Experience with qualitative analysis, particularly using NVivo15 software, is preferred but not required. Attention to detail and the ability to clean and manage data effectively is essential. Strong written and verbal communication skills for collaborating with nursing professionals. Ability to work independently, manage tasks efficiently, and meet deadlines with minimal supervision.",
      "department": "Frances Payne Bolton School of Nursing (STUDENT)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190642": {
      "title": "Student Services Office Assistant Position",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-26 Location: Case Western Reserve University: STUDENT EMPLOYMENT: Frances Payne Bolton School of Nursing (STUDENT) The FPB School of Nursing Student Services Department is seeking a motivated, dependable, and team-oriented undergraduate student to join our office as a Federal Work Study Student status is required . This position provides valuable hands-on experience in a professional office environment while supporting the day-to-day operations of the Student Services team. The student will assist with a variety of administrative, outreach, and event-related responsibilities that contribute to a vibrant and welcoming campus community. Work will primarily take place in the Health Education Campus (HEC), with some remote work opportunities available 2 . Key Responsibilities Office Administration Assist with general office duties including filing, photocopying, scanning, and organizing documents Draft, proofread, and send emails on behalf of the department as directed Support staff with scheduling, meeting preparation, and note-taking as needed Assist with the preparation and distribution of newsletters and departmental communications Maintain an organized and professional office environment Event Planning & Logistics Assist Student Staff with the planning, coordination, and execution of Student Services events and programming Support event set-up and clean-up, including arranging furniture, materials, and signage Help manage event logistics such as greeting and directing guests, monitoring attendance, and coordinating seating arrangements Assist with the preparation of event materials, name tags, programs, and other supplies Outreach & Communications Engage in outreach efforts to students, faculty, and staff via email and social media platforms Assist in creating and scheduling social media content to promote Student Services programs and events Help maintain department communication channels to ensure timely and accurate information sharing General Support Attend departmental meetings and assist as needed Contribute to newsletter content creation and editing Perform other duties as assigned by the supervising staff member Work schedule is 10-15 hours per week for Fall 2026 and Spring 2027 semesters. Hourly rate range is $13.81 - $15.00 per hour Required Skills & Qualifications Currently enrolled as an undergraduate student at the university Must be eligible for Federal Work Study (as determined by the Financial Aid Office) 1 Must have One (1) Year of related experience in working in an Office setting Strong written and verbal communication skills Ability to work collaboratively as part of a team Dependable and punctual with a strong sense of responsibility Conscientious and detail-oriented with a commitment to accuracy Ability to manage multiple tasks and prioritize effectively Comfortable using standard office technology including Microsoft Office Suite, email platforms, and social media tools Professional demeanor and positive attitude when interacting with students, staff, and faculty Please submit a current resume and a brief statement of interest. Applicants must be eligible for Federal Work Study.",
      "department": "Frances Payne Bolton School of Nursing (STUDENT)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/183597": {
      "title": "Mandel School Student Ambassador",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-05-12 Location: Mandel School of Applied Social Sciences The purpose of the Mandel School Student Ambassadors position is to support the efforts to recruit and retain a high quality and diverse student body for all formats of the Mandel School’s programs (Masters of Social Work: Weekly, Online and Weekend format, Dual Degree students as well as the MNO and Certificate of Nonprofit Management). The foremost consideration in our office is to meet the needs of our prospective and admitted students. This position is responsible for efforts regarding student outreach and engagement. Ambassadors must share in the mission and goals of the Admissions Office and the Mandel School. Primary Duties and Responsibilities: 1. Provide information regarding the admission process, program structure and strengths, curriculum options and general questions about the MSW, MNO and dual degree p rograms to prospective students and their support system. Weekly Student Ambassadors (MSW and MNO): Summer 2026 Meet with visiting students to the School lead tours of the Mandel School and Mandel Center accompany student/guest(s) to lunch or coffee to discuss the Mandel experience Participate in group Zoom webinars throughout the summer Engage in individual outreach and support (call, email, text, Zoom, chat) of admitted students per an assigned “caseload” to prevent summer melt Fall 2026 Meet with visiting students to the School lead tours of the Mandel School and Mandel Center accompany student/guest(s) to lunch or coffee to discuss their Mandel experience Participate in monthly evening Zoom webinars Based on availability attend several (not all) webinars during the semester Spring 2027 Engage in individual outreach and support (call, email, text, Zoom, chat) of admitted students per an assigned “caseload” attend Open House Saturday, February 28, 2026, all day (place hold on Friday as well for reception and event preparation) Weekend MSW Student Ambassadors Summer 2026 Participate in select Admissions Office Information Sessions/Webinars on Zoom ,7 pm. Attend in person Saturday, June7 Weekend Experience recruitment event. Fall 2026 Lead tours of the Mandel School and Mandel Center Accompany student to lunch or coffee to discuss their Mandel experience. Participate in select Admissions Office Information Sessions on Zoom. Spring 2027 Participate in select Admissions Office Information Sessions on Zoom (certain set dates for fall and spring semesters, all held at 7 pm). Engage in individual outreach and support (call, email, text, Zoom) of admitted students per an assigned “caseload”. Online MSW Student Ambassadors Summer 2026 Participate in evening Admissions Office Information Sessions/Webinars on Zoom. Engage in individual outreach and support (call, email, text, Zoom, chat) of admitted students per an assigned “caseload” to prevent summer melt. Fall 2026 Participate in evening Admissions Office Information Sessions/ Webinars on Zoom. Connect with newly admitted online students to answer any questions they have about the program. Spring 2027 Participate in evening Admissions Office Information Sessions/ Webinars on Zoom. Connect with newly admitted online students to answer questions they may have about the program. If in Cleveland area - attend Open House Saturday, February 28, 2026, all day. 2. Be trained to use the Mandel School’s CRM Slate. Document all interactions with prospective, applied and admitted students in the system. 3. Office projects to be completed while working in the office. Skills and Qualifications: ● Demonstrated passion for the Mandel School and Case Western Reserve University. ● Excellent interpersonal, customer service skills. ● Ability to be self-motivated and flexible while working in an open concept space. ● Strong written and verbal communication skills. ● Respond promptly to requests to support campus visits from the Admissions Coordinator. ● Ability to work with accuracy and attention to detail. ● Previous experience in an admissions office or other related higher education based environment is ideal yet not required. ● At all times and in all situations, represent CWRU and the Admissions Department in a professional, friendly and honest manner. Work Capabilities: Candidates must have access to a computer and phone to conduct Zoom meetings. Time Commitment: ● This is a part time position. The hours will vary as prospective students request appointments, Information Sessions are scheduled and projects posted. Timely responses to students is critical. Some evening and or weekend hours will be necessary to allow for event participation. ● Mandel School Ambassadors must attend established trainings. Reporting and Supervision: Daily tasks and projects assignments will be managed by both the Assistant Director of Admissions and Manager, Enrollment and Recruitment. Ambassadors may also receive assignments from any staff member in the office. Compensation: The Student Ambassador position is paid at an hourly rate of $13.00 and is responsible for accurately reporting hours worked at the end of each shift in HCM. Retroactive approval of hours will not be authorized. Ambassadors are paid on a bi-weekly basis and must set up direct deposit or pick up checks from the university’s Office of Student Employment. Applicants for the Mandel School Student Ambassador position should demonstrate the following: Current student in good academic standing in the Mandel School at Case Western Reserve University . Enthusiasm for representing the Mandel School and sharing personal experiences with prospective students. Strong interpersonal, communication, and customer service skills. Ability to communicate professionally through phone, email, text, Zoom, and in-person interactions. Dependable, organized, and able to manage responsibilities independently. Comfortable working with diverse student populations and maintaining confidentiality and professionalism. Ability to respond promptly to Admissions Office requests and student inquiries. Willingness to participate in trainings, events, webinars, tours, and outreach activities. Ability to learn and use the Mandel School CRM system (Slate) to document student interactions. Access to a reliable computer, internet connection, and phone for virtual engagement and Zoom meetings. Previous admissions, student leadership, customer service, mentoring, or higher education experience is preferred but not required. Application Instructions Interested students should submit a current resume. MSW students should include their current path of study and practicum experience on their resume. MNO students should submit an updated professional resume highlighting relevant academic, leadership, and professional experience. Student Ambassadors are expected to attend required trainings and maintain professional communication with prospective and admitted students throughout the academic year.",
      "department": "Jack, Joseph and Morton Mandel School of Applied Social Sciences (STUDENT)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188420": {
      "title": "Harris Library Circulation Student Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-10 Deadline: 2026-09-21 Location: Jack Joseph Mandel School of Applied Social Sciences Hiring Western Reserve University undergraduate students with Federal Work-Study only. Hourly rate $13.44 Work at the Circulation desk using computerized library system. Job has a strong emphasis on customer service and being reliable. Student are required to do shelving, photocopying, scanning and other tasks assigned. Strong verbal communication skills essentials. Ability to follow directions. Responsible for closing the library. Hiring for these days and times below: Wednesdays 10:00am-1:00pm Thursdays 10:00am-1:00pm Hiring Case Western Reserve University Undergraduate Students with Federal Work-Study. Basic computer knowledge required. Knowledge of Microsoft Word desirable. Follow directions throughly. Hiring for these days and times below: Wednesdays 10:00am-1:00pm Thursdays 10:00am-1:00pm Hiring Case Western Reserve University Undergraduate Students with Federal Work-Study. Hiring Case Western Reserve University undergraduate students with Federal Work-Study only.",
      "department": "Jack, Joseph and Morton Mandel School of Applied Social Sciences (STUDENT)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/177805": {
      "title": "Biomedical Engineering Department Administrative & Technology Support (STUDENT)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-02-10 Location: Wickenden 340 The Biomedical Engineering Department Administrative & Technology Support (STUDENT) will provide administrative and technology support to the Department of Biomedical Engineering, Case School of Engineering & School of Medicine. We are seeking a detail-oriented student assistant to support departmental organization and digital workflow improvements. Responsibilities include assisting with building and maintaining the department SharePoint site, updating Microsoft SmartForms, organizing department OneDrive folders, and helping organize departmental Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs). Flexible schedule, 10-20 hours per week preferred. $13-18/hour depending on experience. Key Responsibilities: · Support departmental organization · Implement digital workflow improvements and updates · Build and maintain the department SharePoint site · Build and maintain department Microsoft 365 (access) groups · Update Microsoft SmartForms · Organize department OneDrive folders · Organize department SOPs ·         Excellent organizational skills with keen attention to detail ·         Tech-savvy, able to work independently, and comfortable learning new Microsoft 365 tools ·         Strong attention to detail ·         Strong written and verbal communication skills ·         Ability to multitask and prioritize responsibilities effectively ·         Professional demeanor and ability to work in a team-oriented environment Contact Holly Green, Executive Director, Department of Biomedical Engineering: hxg241@case.edu",
      "department": "School of Medicine (STUDENT)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/180820": {
      "title": "Student Receptionist Concierge – Midtown Collaboration Center (MCC)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-04-02 Location: Midtown Collaboration Center (MCC) The Midtown Collaboration Center (MCC) is seeking a detail-oriented, professional, and proactive Student Receptionist Concierge to support daily front desk operations and ensure a welcoming, organized, and well-maintained environment for guests, tenants, and staff. This role is ideal for a student who enjoys working in a dynamic, collaborative setting and can effectively balance customer service, administrative support, and facility coordination. The pay rate for this position is $15 /hour. Key Responsibilities Front Desk & Guest Experience Serve as the first point of contact by greeting and assisting guests, tenants, and visitors Answer general inquiries and direct guests appropriately Ensure all non-event visitors sign in upon arrival Communicate building policies and procedures to guests and staff Administrative & Communication Support Monitor and respond to the IPCHFrontDesk email alias, including sharing announcements Manage room scheduling through the EMS system, including approving requests Update and maintain daily event information on digital displays Assist with scheduling and communication across teams and MCC partners Facilities & Operations Support Conduct daily checks of café, conference rooms, classrooms, and shared spaces to ensure: Cleanliness and organization Proper room setup (chairs, tables, whiteboards, etc.) Adequate supplies are stocked Maintain kitchen and shared spaces, including light cleaning and restocking supplies Report excessive messes, maintenance issues, or concerns to designated staff Mail, Packages & Coordination Check and distribute mail daily Coordinate package pickups in collaboration with the MCC front desk Maintain regular communication with MCC partners regarding events, guests, and logistics Event, Technology & Access Support Provide basic troubleshooting for office equipment (printers, projectors, etc.) Assist with general technology troubleshooting in meeting and event spaces; escalate unresolved issues as needed Support access control, including assisting with badges, door access, and coordinating with security when necessary Coordinate access for events occurring outside standard building hours Additional Responsibilities Print and post weekly room signage (door inserts/cards) Support special projects and additional office tasks as assigned",
      "department": "School of Medicine (STUDENT)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/181174": {
      "title": "Software Engineer (Student) - Medical Imaging Analysis & Machine Learning",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-15 Location: University Hospitals Cleveland Medical Center/Remote We are looking for a student Software Engineer/ Biomedical Engineer with a strong interest in the development, maintenance, and validation of software tools used for ophthalmic image analysis, clinical trials, and AI research initiatives. Working under the guidance of the Computer Vision Scientist and EIARC leadership, this role focuses on building and supporting image analysis software, data pipelines, visualization tools, and validation workflows that enable reliable and efficient reading center operations. The position provides hands-on exposure to clinical research, AI-driven imaging analytics, and real-world healthcare data systems. This role is ideal for students interested in computer science, biomedical engineering, medical imaging, or clinical research, with an interest in ophthalmology and translational AI. The role offers remote or hybrid flexibility and opportunities for publication and collaboration with clinical and engineering experts. Responsibilities · Supporting Imaging preprocessing, registration, segmentation, and feature extraction tools · Assisting with data preparation and validation necessary for clinical-grade image analysis · Perform software verification and validation (V&V) to ensure accuracy, robustness, and reproducibility of imaging and ML outputs. · Validate algorithms against ground truth annotations, clinical reference standards, or expert-reviewed datasets Document software designs, validation results, and workflows to support research publications, regulatory alignment, and translational use Salary Range: $14 – $18 (Experience + Relevant Coursework) · Strong organizational skills with careful attention to detail · Effective interpersonal and communication skills, both written and verbal · General computer proficiency, including basic to intermediate use of Microsoft Word, Excel, and Outlook · Ability to manage multiple tasks while maintaining accuracy and confidentiality Preferred Skills · Strong written communication and editing skills · Familiarity with clinical research environments · Interest in medical research, imaging, or healthcare operations Working Conditions · Primarily office-based, with periodic time spent in clinical or research office environments No unusual physical demands or safety hazards associated with this position Please submit Your resume A brief cover letter describing your interests Any relevant project links (GitHub, portfolio, publications) We are looking for students that will be able to work full time over the summer and then available to work part-time over the upcoming academic year. Email juri.moon@case.edu",
      "department": "School of Medicine (STUDENT)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/181582": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - (Student)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-04-14 Location: Cleveland, OH Position Description We are looking for energetic, dependable research assistants to work with our study team on a National Institute of Health study of the relationships among sleep, fatigue, and functioning among 11-14 year-old adolescents in greater Cleveland. Responsibilities include: ·      Recruiting participants in greater Cleveland ·      Visiting families in their homes and interviewing a parent and child. ·      Entering data into study databases Time: 10-20 hrs per week during the academic year. 40 hours in the summer Horuly Rate: $15-16.50/hr Requirements: ·      Excellent interpersonal skills ·      Must be comfortable interviewing in home settings ·      Attention to detail ·      A valid driver’s license and car to conduct home interviews Summer 2026 (up to 40 hrs) Fall Semester 2026 (up to 20 hrs) Requirements: ·      Excellent interpersonal skills ·      Must be comfortable interviewing in home settings ·      Attention to detail ·      A valid driver’s license and car to conduct home interviews",
      "department": "School of Medicine (STUDENT)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/182350": {
      "title": "Events Assistant Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-04-25 Location: Sears Tower T101 Job Title: Event Assistant Coordinator Department: School of Medicine Alumni and Development Position Summary The Event Assistant Coordinator helps the Development and Alumni team at Case Western Reserve University with all parts of planning and running events. This role is for a student who wants to help behind the scenes and on-site to make sure every event is a success. You will help us find event supporters, talk to donors, and make sure our guests have a great experience. Key Responsibilities Special Projects for Events Goal-Oriented Tasks: Work on various projects designed to improve and benefit our upcoming events. Research: Look into new ideas, venues, or community partners that can help our events grow. Planning Support: Assist with the early stages of project development to ensure every event has what it needs to succeed. On-Site Event Support Setup and Greeting: Help set up event spaces, staff the registration table, and greet guests as they arrive. Event Logistics: Stay on-site during events to help with any needs and make sure everything runs smoothly. Campus Errands: Carry out various errands across the CWRU campus to pick up or deliver event materials. Gaining Event Supporters Finding Donors: Reach out to local businesses and partners to find supporters and donors for event prizes. Increasing Attendance: Help get more people to attend by sending friendly reminders and making follow-up calls. Local Promotion: Help spread the word about events to the campus and the local community. Saying Thank You: Help write thank-you notes and find simple ways to recognize the people who support our events. Technical and Office Tasks Office Technology: Use standard office software and tools to help organize event details and communications. Flexible Work: Complete event prep tasks in the office or remotely depending on the project needs. Pay: $15 per hour Desired Qualities Reliability: Someone who will show up to the office and work on a regular basis. Positive Attitude: A friendly, upbeat person who enjoys greeting and helping others. Self-Starter: A proactive team member who is ready to jump in and get things done. Bold Communicator: Comfortable and confident when reaching out to businesses to request donations. Tech-Savvy: Able to use basic office tools and handle tasks both in-person and remotely. Professional: Polite and well-spoken when representing the University to alumni and donors. Letter of Interest and Resume",
      "department": "School of Medicine (STUDENT)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/182621": {
      "title": "Standardized Patient / Native Spanish Speaker",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-04-30 Location: CWRU University School of Medicine Samson Pavilion HEC Student Standardized Patient (SPs) are hired on an as needed basis for the programs oulined below.  We are unable to guaranteed hours but all SPs are paid $25/hr for both training and programs. However, if you demonstrate an affinity for the work, and your schedule allows, you might be added to the general SP pool and recruited for programs in English (M-F between 8-5 pm) as needed. Our standardized patient (SP) program hires people from the univeristiy and community to portray a variety of patient roles with specific medical symptoms as well as to act as body models for physical exam and ultrasound sessions. Each SP performance is standardized to portray a “real” patient case accurately and reliably over time and across different SPs. No acting experience is required but SPs may be specifically trained to memorize a script and assume the “patient’s” history, emotional characteristics, physical symptoms, and other body language. All SPs must be 18+ years old We are in need of native Spanish Speakers for our Medical Spanish Electives.   These programs are taking place in the Samson Pavilion - Health Education Campus. The first opportunity will be a PA Medical Spanish elective (Physician Assistant) on Thursday Mornings between 10 am and 12:30 pm from June to the 2nd week of July (interview only with some light physical exam, no hospital gowns).  Depending on the case demographic SPs may only work one or two of the dates. During the fall semester the School of Medicine will run an Intermediate Medical Spanish Elective (Mid-Term and Final exams using SPs it is often held on weekday afternoons).  Historically these are interviews only with no physical exam. In addition, we hold an Advanced Medical Spanish Elective (held early evening on weekdays) with bi monthly medical Spanish simulations which can be interview only, a gowned physical exam (you have on sport shorts/bra under a cloth hospital gown) or a focused interview and physical exam.  Each date will have a short SP Case to learn (the patient's name, history of current illness, past medical history and any physical exam findings - for example it hurts when they press on your abdomen. )  All cases are trained via zoom by arrangement the week before the program. If, after checking your Summer/Fall schedule, you find you have availbitly for the above programs, feel free to apply!  If you have any questions feel free to email me at hmg29@case.edu.",
      "department": "School of Medicine (STUDENT)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/182946": {
      "title": "School of Medicine Near Peer Tutor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-05-06 Location: Health Education Building The Near-Peer Tutor (NPT) is a medical student further along in the CWRU School of Medicine program (M3 or M4) who supports earlier-stage medical students in both academic content and study skills development. This role is offered as a paid position or as part of the Near-Peer Tutoring Program and is supervised by the Learning Specialist Team and Faculty Advisor within the Office of Student Affairs. $20 hr Successful completion of M2 year Successful completion of Step 1 Letter of recommendation from a faculty member and/or learning specialist",
      "department": "School of Medicine (STUDENT)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/183612": {
      "title": "Student Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-05-13 Location: MSTP Office, School of Medicine The Medical Scientist Training Program (MSTP) is looking for a student to serve as a part-time office assistant. This student will support all MSTP office staff, reporting to the Director of Administrative Operations. Projects may include: Scan documents, archive files, and enter data Pack and mail promotional materials Set up program events and arrange catering when needed Update and maintain website Deliver paperwork to campus offices Other office projects as needed The office assistant requires 8-15 hours/week commitment for Summer 2026. Work hours are typically within the hours of 9 a.m. and 4 p.m., Monday through Friday. Pay is $12.35/hour. Critical thinker who can work independently with a strong attention to detail; High degree of organization and accuracy; able to maintain confidentiality and professionalism at all times, and self-motivated and willing to learn. Requires knowledge in both Microsoft Suite and GSuite, including Microsoft Word, Microsoft Excel, and Gmail, Google Spreadsheets. Please contact MSTP (mstp@case.edu) with any questions regarding the application, position requirements and/or job responsibilities.",
      "department": "School of Medicine (STUDENT)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188923": {
      "title": "Communications and Events Student Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-05 Location: Mary Ann Swetland Center for Environmental Health The Mary Ann Swetland Center for Environmental Health in the School of Medicine seeks a motivated and detail-oriented student assistant to support center-wide communications, event planning, and digital engagement. The ideal candidate will help amplify the Center’s impact through creative content development, event support, and data-driven outreach strategies. The candidate must be currently enrolled as a student at Case Western Reserve University to be eligible for the position. Hours: 20 hours per week, flexible scheduling to accommodate academic commitments. Students may work virtually for up to 10 hours per week. Pay Rate: $15.00-$22.14 per hour depending on experience and training. Location: CWRU Institute for Population and Community Health, Warren E. Anderson MidTown Collaboration Center, 1974 East 66th Street, Suite 300, Cleveland, Ohio 44103 • Strong written and verbal communication skills: Ability to write clearly and concisely for diverse audiences. • Experience with digital communication tools (e.g., Constant Contact, Canva, or similar platforms). • Familiarity with social media management and content creation. • Basic graphic design skills (using Canva, Adobe Creative Suite, or similar). • Strong organizational and project management abilities: Able to manage multiple tasks and deadlines. • Attention to detail and ability to proofread and edit content. • Analytical skills: Comfortable interpreting website and newsletter analytics. • Experience with event planning or coordination (preferred but not required). • Basic understanding of website structure and usability; experience with website content management systems (e.g., WordPress) is a plus. • Professionalism, reliability, and ability to work independently as well as part of a team. Please submit a resume, cover letter expressing your interest and fit with the position, one sample of communications work (e.g., flyer, newsletter, social media), and a proposed work schedule.",
      "department": "School of Medicine (STUDENT)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189348": {
      "title": "Student Genomic Data Analyst",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-13 Location: Wolstein Research Building Position Type: Undergraduate Research Assistant Department: Genetics and Genome Sciences, School of Medicine Principal Investigator: Thomas LaFramboise, PhD Time Commitment: Flexible; minimum 10 hours per week recommended, up to 20 hours Compensation: $15/hour About the Laboratory Our laboratory is a computational cancer genomics research group in the CWRU School of Medicine. We use cutting-edge DNA sequencing technologies to study the molecular basis of cancer, with the goal of identifying the causes of malignancy, discovering biomarkers that predict patient outcomes and treatment response, and characterizing the genomic landscape of hematological and other cancers. Our work sits at the intersection of biology, medicine, computer science, and statistics — and we are looking for motivated undergraduate students to join our team. About the Position This is a hands-on research position in which you will contribute meaningfully to ongoing scientific projects from day one. You will work directly with real genomic data from cancer patients — including DNA sequence data generated from tumor samples — and will play an active role in the analysis, interpretation, and communication of findings. You will be mentored by the Principal Investigator and will work closely with a graduate student in the laboratory, gaining exposure to the full arc of the scientific research process. This position is ideal for students interested in pursuing careers or graduate training in computational biology, bioinformatics, genomics, medicine, data science, or related fields. Responsibilities Data analysis and scripting: Write and execute scripts in Python, R, and/or Unix/bash to process, analyze, and manage large-scale genomic datasets derived from cancer patient samples Data interpretation: Analyze and interpret results from computational pipelines, drawing biologically and clinically meaningful conclusions from complex datasets Data visualization: Create high-quality figures and visualizations that accurately and clearly summarize data characteristics and analytical findings for use in manuscripts and presentations Scientific writing: Participate in the drafting and revision of scientific manuscripts for submission to peer-reviewed journals Literature review: Regularly read and critically evaluate primary scientific literature to deepen your understanding of ongoing projects and the broader field of cancer genomics Collaboration: Work closely with a graduate student mentor, other laboratory members, and researchers in collaborating labs in a collaborative, supportive research environment Presentation: Present your work at laboratory meetings, and — as projects mature — at internal research symposia and external scientific conference Current Research Projects Undergraduate researchers in our laboratory may contribute to one or more of the following active projects, depending on their interests and skills: Drug response biomarker discovery: Mining large-scale genomic databases to identify DNA sequence features that predict patient response to specific cancer therapies, with the goal of enabling more precise, personalized treatment Viral involvement in malignancy: Investigating the role of viral sequences and viral-associated mutational patterns in the development and progression of cancer, using computational approaches to detect viral signatures in patient sequencing data Additional projects in mutational signature analysis, epigenomics, and cancer genomics may also be available Opportunities We are committed to the professional development of every member of our laboratory. Undergraduate researchers in our group have access to the following opportunities: Conference presentations: Outstanding work will be presented at research symposia and scientific conferences, giving you experience communicating science to expert audiences Manuscript authorship: Students who make substantial contributions to research projects will be included as authors on peer-reviewed publications — a significant distinction that strengthens applications to graduate and medical school Mentorship: You will receive dedicated mentorship from both graduate students and the PI, with guidance on career planning, graduate school applications, and scientific development Skill development: You will gain practical, marketable skills in bioinformatics, computational data analysis, scientific writing, and genomic medicine Current enrollment as an undergraduate student at CWRU Experience with programming in Python, R, or Unix/bash Coursework in biology, genetics, biochemistry, computer science, statistics, mathematics, or a related field Strong interest in cancer biology, genomics, computational biology, or data science Enthusiasm, curiosity, and a strong work ethic Ability to work both independently and collaboratively",
      "department": "School of Medicine (STUDENT)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190475": {
      "title": "Graduate Student Research Assistant (Nutrition)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-25 Location: Cleveland, OH Nourishing Tomorrow (NT) is a NIH-funded, community-based research project led by Case Western Reserve University in partnership with MetroHealth, University Hospitals, and the Greater Cleveland Food Bank. The study evaluates approaches to supporting pregnant people and their families through increased access to nutritious food and supportive resources during pregnancy and postpartum. The Graduate Research Assistant (GRA) will support participant engagement and data collection for Nourishing Tomorrow. The primary responsibility of this position is conducting standardized participant interviews, by phone, at key study timepoints, requiring both careful adherence to research protocols and compassionate, attentive interviewing, particularly when addressing sensitive topics. As a primary point of contact for participants, the Research Assistant plays an important role in establishing trust, supporting retention, and ensuring high-quality study data. Essential Functions Schedule and conduct standardized participant interviews at baseline, near delivery, and three months postpartum, following study protocols and scripts to ensure consistent and high-quality data collection. Interviews include sensitive topics related to psychosocial well-being and require compassionate, attentive interviewing, sound judgment, and the ability to recognize participant responses or behaviors that may require immediate follow-up. Coordinate participant data collection activities, including scheduling follow-up interviews and 24-hour dietary recalls with the BioNutrition team, tracking completion of required study activities, and communicating reminders and follow-up information to participants. Process participant incentive payments following completion of study activities and maintain accurate documentation of payments in accordance with study and university procedures. Support participant engagement and retention through approved phone, email, and text communication. Establish and maintain respectful, responsive relationships with participants and assist in identifying and addressing barriers to completion of study activities. Identify and respond to participant psychological safety concerns that arise during study interviews, including elevated depression symptoms, suicidal ideation, or other signs of psychological distress. Follow established safety protocols, promptly communicate concerns to designated study leadership, and support timely documentation and reporting of adverse events and notification of clinical/hospital partners as required. Enter and maintain participants and study data in REDCap and other approved systems; review records for completeness and accuracy and assist with routine data quality assurance and cleaning activities. Maintain accurate and timely study documentation and participant tracking records in accordance with study protocols, IRB requirements, and institutional policies. Maintain participant confidentiality and protect sensitive research information in accordance with human subjects research requirements, study protocols, and institutional policies. Communicate professionally and respectfully with participants, research staff, clinical partners, the BioNutrition team, and community partners to support coordinated study implementation. Participate in study team meetings, interviewer training, protocol training, quality assurance activities, and ongoing refresher training to maintain consistency and fidelity in participant data collection. Participant Support: Build supportive and respectful relationships with study participants while maintaining appropriate professional boundaries. Approach participant interactions with empathy, cultural humility, and sensitivity to circumstances that may affect pregnant and postpartum individuals and their families. Listen attentively for indications that a participant may be experiencing distress or need additional support and respond in accordance with established study protocols. Provide study-approved resources or referrals when appropriate and directed by study procedures. Apply principles from Mental Health First Aid or similar training when appropriate, while recognizing the boundaries of the research role and connecting participants with appropriate professional resources, including informing study personnel as per study protocols. Training Requirements Selected candidates will complete required institutional and study-specific training before independently performing study activities. Training may include research ethics, confidentiality, study protocols, REDCap, participant engagement, and other project-specific procedures. Mental Health First Aid training or certification is preferred and may be provided or required based on the responsibilities of the position. Hours and Pay The GRA will work approximately 10–20 hours per week with flexible hours. Work can be done remotely or at a PRCHN or Department location, with occasional in-person meetings or activities as needed. The pay range is $18–22/hour depending on experience. Required Qualifications Current graduate student in psychology, anthropology, nutrition, public health, health sciences, or a related field. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Strong organizational skills and attention to detail. Ability to work independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team. Ability to maintain confidentiality and handle sensitive participant information appropriately. Ability to interact respectfully with individuals from diverse backgrounds. Preferred Qualifications Experience working with research participants, patients, families, or community members. Experience in community-based research, public health, nutrition, healthcare, or social services. Experience working with populations experiencing food insecurity or other social and economic barriers to health. Experience with REDCap or other electronic data collection systems. Experience completing Mental Health First Aid (MHFA) training or certification. Experience working as part of a multidisciplinary or community-academic team. Applicants should submit a CV/resume and a brief cover letter explaining their interest in the position and relevant experience. Experience can include but is not limited to coursework, employment, internships, volunteer experiences, and other research experience. Please also provide contact information for two professional references. All materials must be submitted via Interfolio.",
      "department": "School of Medicine (STUDENT)"
    },
