    "https://apply.interfolio.com/178183": {
      "title": "PostDoc",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-02-18 Location: Bio Research Building The Postdoctoral Scholar will lead innovative research projects focused on the intersection of advanced computational methods development, multi-omics modeling, and complex inflammatory disease biology with special emphasis on sepsis endotyping, as well as other research areas and disease applications in the Brubaker Lab. The position offers opportunity for advanced training in interdisciplinary science, translational research, and research computing. The scholar will design and execute methods for patient stratification using multi-modal immunophenotyping and clinical data toward the goal of transforming the diagnosis and management of sepsis and other acute critical care illnesses. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS Technology and Methodology Development: Independently develop and optimize novel coonalmputati and analytical methods, including unsupervised learning, classifier development and testing, and development of advanced multi-modal data integration and analysis techniques. (50%). Data Integration and Analysis: Conduct rigorous analysis of multimodal data across the scholar’s primary project, support other funded projects in the lab, and develop new directions of interest to the Scholar’s career development. (20%) Collaboration and Mentorship: Collaborate within an interdisciplinary research environment, provide mentorship to junior lab personnel, and actively participate in team-driven research projects (20%). NONESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS Lab Organization and Other Duties: Assist in the general maintenance of the laboratory, development of grant proposals and manuscripts, and organization as needed (10%). CONTACTS Department: Daily interaction with the principal investigator and lab members to discuss ongoing projects and results. University: Regular contact with collaborators across multiple departments and centers within the university. Occasional communication with administrative support and research offices. External: Occasional interactions with external collaborators, vendors, institutions, and funding agencies. Students: Regular interactions and mentorship of graduate and undergraduate students. SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITIES Provide mentorship and guidance to graduate students, research assistants, and other junior lab personnel. WORKING CONDITIONS The employee may be exposed to bloodborne pathogens and infectious materials. Personal protective equipment (gloves, masks, eye protection) will be provided. Occasional weekend and evening work may be required depending on project needs. In compliance with the City of Cleveland’s Pay Transparency Ordinance (effective October 27, 2025), the annual starting base salary range for this position is $55,000 - $62,652. CWRU considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Postdocs receive individual and family healthcare benefits, life insurance, and employee assistance. Education: PhD in Biomedical Sciences, Bioengineering, Computational Biology, or a related discipline. Experience: Demonstrated experience with developing advanced computational workflows to handle heterogeneous biological datasets. REQUIRED SKILLS 1.       Experience developing or utilizing advanced computational modeling techniques on heterogeneous biological datasets. 2.       Proficiency in single-cell RNA sequencing, high-dimensional data analysis, bioinformatics tools, and programming languages (R, Python). 3.       Demonstrated ability to independently design, execute, and troubleshoot complex workflows. 4.       Excellent analytical and problem-solving skills; ability to interpret and integrate complex datasets. 5.       Strong organizational skills, ability to manage multiple projects simultaneously, and prioritize tasks effectively. 6.       Strong record of peer-reviewed scientific publications. 7.       Excellent written and oral communication skills; capable of clearly presenting complex scientific data and collaborating effectively within interdisciplinary teams. 8.       Ability to work effectively independently and collaboratively within a team. Must be highly motivated, responsible, dependable, and a self-starter. 9.       Commitment to fostering a collaborative and inclusive research environment. 10.   Ability to work with sensitive information and maintain confidentiality. 11.   Familiarity with policies and procedures of lab and adhere to chemical and biological safety and manage them. 12.   Ability to meet consistent attendance. In compliance with the City of Cleveland’s Pay Transparency Ordinance (effective October 27, 2025), the annual starting base salary range for this position is $55,000 - $62,652. CWRU considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Postdocs receive individual and family healthcare benefits, life insurance, and employee assistance.",
      "department": "Pathology (PD)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/178564": {
      "title": "Postdoc",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-02-25 Location: Wolstein Research Building This is a key position within the lab and will be responsible for planning and execution of experiments in close collaboration with the Principal Investigator. The lab is working closely together with other PI's in Pathology and school of medicine including collaborations with a multidisciplinary team of biologists, chemists, physicists and clinicians. The qualified candidate will prepare manuscripts and contribute preliminary data, background and significance and experimental design for grant proposals. The focus of the current position is acute myeloid leukemia. Goal is to discover novel molecular mechanisms that could be targeted to develop new therapeutic approaches. Regulation of cellular signaling by hexosamine biosynthetic pathway, O-GlcNAcylation and NF-kB transcription pathways are key areas of attention. The work will involve experimental techniques ranging from basic cell and molecular biology, immunology, biochemistry to cell culture, animal studies and patient samples. Experience with mouse models and flow cytometry is required. The candidate will work independently and should be competent to read the literature, assist with designing and planning of projects and carry out research on the assigned project. Expected to apply/secure training fellowships. TECHNICAL SKILLS 1.   Design, plan and carry out experiments in collaboration with PI. Provide assistance and work closely with staff, post-doctoral scholars and graduate students ensuring projects are being completed according to the research plan. Advise/Assist in developing improved techniques, project methods, or procedures. 2.   Oversee general lab operations including inventory, lab organization and maintain certain equipment/facilities. Must demonstrate compliance with policies, procedures and regulations of the Animal Resource Center, IACUC, Animal Welfare Act, Public Health Service Policy, AAALAC guidelines and other applicable regulatory guidelines for which essential training will be provided. 3.   Analyze and record results. Assist in manuscript preparation. 4.   Perform/supervise rodent husbandry including breeding, genotyping and maintain an accurate animal database. 5.   Present results in laboratory meetings and other scientific meetings. 6.   Perform other duties as assigned. DESIRABLE SKILLS 1.   Ability to work well with others and to work independently strictly following directions and protocols. 2.   Ability to collect and analyze data with attention to detail and do multiple tasks simultaneously. 3.   Effective oral and written communication skills.  Must be able to effectively communicate with the PI/supervisor as well as other lab members. 4.   Good interpersonal skills; ability to work and communicate with various individuals within and external to the University. 5.   Good organization skills; ability to multi-task, prioritize and meet deadlines.  Must demonstrate attention to detail and accuracy and follow-through on assigned tasks. 6.   Ability to work effectively independently and collaboratively within a team. 7.   Ability to work with sensitive information and maintain confidentiality. 8.   Good problem-solving skills; must demonstrate good analytical skills, sound judgment and good decision-making. 9.   Ability to rely on and follow instructions and pre-established guidelines to perform the functions of the job. 10. Ability to operate laboratory equipment and learn to operate new instruments 11. Familiarity with policies and procedures of lab and chemical safety 12. Knowledge in Microsoft Office, database applications, and with internet navigation. 13. Willingness to learn new techniques, procedures, processes, and software programs as needed. 14. Ability to organize, maintain and prepare accurate records 15. Must be willing to adjust working hours according to the nature of ongoing experiments and many times work extended hours to finish the experiments. Working on weekends and holidays maybe required depending on the ongoing experiments and need to accelerate data generation and manuscript and grant preparations. 16. Must demonstrate flexibility and ability to work under pressure; must be able and willing to conform to shifting priorities, demands and timeline. 17. Self driven effort in terms of thinking, planning, and working is expected. EDUCATION REQUIREMENTS Ph.D in biological sciences with hands-on experience in Immunology, Biochemistry, Cell Biology and mouse work. YEARS OF RELEVANT EXPERIENCE REQUIRED Ph.D with 0-2 years of postdoctoral experience and demonstrated research ability in the form of lead author publications. Higher experience will be considered for Research Associate position. In compliance with the City of Cleveland’s Pay Transparency Ordinance (effective October 27, 2025), the annual starting base salary range for this position is $55,000 - $62,652. CWRU considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Postdocs receive individual and family healthcare benefits, life insurance, and employee assistance.",
      "department": "Pathology (PD)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/168288": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar",
      "description": "Posted: 2025-08-28 Location: Cleveland, Ohio Postdoctoral positions are available in the Stelzer Lab in the Department of Physiology and Biophysics at Case Western Reserve University School of Medicine. We are looking for recent PhD graduates or soon to be PhD graduates who are interested in working on developing small molecule and gene therapy approaches for treating genetic and acquired heart failure. The lab uses mouse models of heart failure, in vivo assessment  of cardiac function, cellular measurements of cardiac muscle and metabolic function to develop new therapies. These opportunities are ideal for candidates interested in a career in academia or industry. The positions are available immediately; however, the start date is flexible PhD in scince field",
      "department": "Physiology & Biophysics (PD)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/180134": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-03-19 Location: Xin Qi Laboratory, Cleveland, Ohio The successful candidate will participate in DOD-funded research projects focused on elucidating the molecular mechanisms of metabolic dysregulation in neurodegenerative diseases and developing potential therapeutics. The lab employs an integrated experimental approach, including in vitro cell culture systems, in vivo transgenic animal models, and patient-derived iPSC neurons and glial cells, to investigate disease pathogenesis. In parallel, the laboratory pursues therapeutic development, using rationally designed peptide inhibitors of protein-protein interactions and high-throughput screening strategies to identify nvel treatment approaches for neurological disorders.  Other responsibilities include: Design, execute, and analyze experiments in models of neurodegenerative disease Perform and optimize molecular, biochemical, and cell-based assays, including work with cell lines, primary cultures, and iPSC-derived neurons and glia. Conduct in vivo studies using transgenic animal models, including tissue collection and downstream analyses, as appropriate Analyze and interpret experimental data, maintain detailed records, and prepare figures and reports. Work on preparation of manuscripts for research findings Collaborate with other lab members and interdisciplinary partners within and ouside the University Particpate in the general scientific activities of the laboratory, inclduing mentoring junior trainees when appropriate Doctorate in neuroscience, molecular biology, cell biology, pharmacology, or a related field 4 years experience with cellular and molecular biology and animal models of disease must be able to work in the United States In compliance with the City of Cleveland's Pay Transparency Ordinance, the annual starting base salary for this position is $55,000-$62,652. CWRU considrs factors such as (but not limited o) the specific grant funding and the terms of the reseach grant  when extending an offer. Postdocs receive individual and family healthcare benefits, life insurance, and employe assistance Interested parties should send a cover letter, CV, PhD certificate, and the  names/phone numbers of three people who could provide letters of refernce by e-mail. https://apply.interfolio.com/183552",
      "department": "Physiology & Biophysics (PD)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/182264": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-04-23 Location:     Cleveland, Ohio Case Western Reserve University, Department of Physiology & Biophysics is seeking a qualified candidate to fill a postdoctoral scholar position. This is a one-year appointment with the possibility of renewing for additional years. The successful candidate will work in the laboratory of Professor Witold Surewicz on a research project exploring the tole of liquid-liquid phase separation in neurodegenerative diseases. Some of our recent papers in this area include: Boyko et al., PNAS 117, 31882-31890 (2020), Babinchak et al., Nature Commun 11:5574 (2020), Boyko and Surewicz, Trends Cell Biol 32, 611-623 (2022), Haider et al., Biophys J 123, 361-373 (2024), Monnaka et al., Commun Chem 9:126 (2026). Responsibilities and Duties: Design and perform experiments related to the research project Interpret experimental data Participate in preparation of manuscripts for publication Minimum Qualifications: Doctorate in Biochemistry, Chemistry, Biophysics or related areas. 0-2 years of postdoctoral experience Excellent written and oral communication skills necessary Prior experience in liquid-liquid phase separation, protein aggregation and/or protein-protein interactions is highly desirable. Send letter of application, curriculum vitae, names and contact information professional references to this opening. Apply to: https://apply.interfolio.com/185403 Review of applications will begin when information is received via interfolio. In compliance with the City of Cleveland’s Pay Transparency Ordinance (effective October 27, 2025), the annual starting base salary range for this position is $55,000 - $63,480. CWRU considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Postdocs receive individual and family healthcare benefits, life insurance, and employee assistance.",
      "department": "Physiology & Biophysics (PD)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190035": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar, Department of Physiology & Biophysics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-20 Location: Shiwei Zhu lab, Cleveland, Ohio The Zhu Lab in the Department of Physiology & Biophysics at Case Western Reserve University is seeking a qualified candidate to fill a postdoctoral scholar position. This is a one-year appointment with the possibility of renewal for additonal years, contingent  upon satisfactory performance and continued funding. The candidate will investigate host defense mechansms and pathogen adaptation within host cells using cutting-edge cryo-electron tomography (cryo-ET) techniques in conjunction with cryo-correlative light and electron microscopy (cryo-CLEM) and cryo-focused ion beam/scanning electron microscopy (cryo-FIB/SEM). This position focuses on elucidating the structural mechanisms of GBP1 (Guanylate Binding Protein 1), a critical interferon-induced immune protein that recognizes and destroys diverse intracellular bacterial pathogens. This postdoctoral fellow will visualize critical biological processes underlying host cell defense against microbial threats using state-of-the-art cryo-ET, and will contribute to developing novel in situ imaging methods for studying host-pathogen interactions at molecular resolution. Other duties and responsibilities include: Design and execute in vitro reconstitution experiments to investigate GBP1 targeting of pathogen surfaces, including protein purification, mutagenesis, and biochemical characterization. Perform cryo-ET sample preparation and data collection, including plunge-freezing, cryo-FIB/SEM lamella millling, and tilt series acquisiton on the Titan Krios cryo-electron microscope. Conduct cryo-CLEM to identify and target biological structures of interest, correlating flourescence signals with high-resolution ultrastructural data in both ex situ and in situ contexts. Analyze cryo-ET datasets, including tomogram reconstruction, subtomogram averaging, and 3D structure determination, to elucidate GBP1 structural mechanisms during pathogen recognition and organelle interactions. Perform mammaliam cell culture, genetic manipulation, and bacterial infection assays to generate cellular models for structural studies. Ph.D. in Structural Biology, Biochemistry, Cell Biology, Microbiology, Immunology, or a related field. Experience with cryo-electron microscopy is preferred but not required; candidates with strong backgrounds in structural biology, biochemistry, or molecular/cell biology and a demonstrated interest in learning cryo-EM techniques will also be considered. Candidates should have at least one peer reviewed publication. Ability to work independently and collaboratively in a multidisciplinary research environment. In compliance with the City of Cleveland's Pay transparency Ordinance (effective October 27, 2025) the annual starting base salary range for this position is $55,000-$62,652. CWRU considers actors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of research gant when extending an offer. Postdocs receive individual and family healthcare benefits, life insurance  and employee assistance. Apply through nterfolio only https://apply.interfolio.com/191863",
      "department": "Physiology & Biophysics (PD)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189118": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar",
      "description": "Posted: 2023-09-02 Deadline: 2026-09-10 Location: Tao Lab, Main Campus The Department of Pharmacology at Case Western Reserve University is seeking a qualified candidate for a Postdoctoral Scholar position in the laboratory of Yongfeng Tao. The scholar will lead chemical biology and chemical proteomics studies to discover small-molecule ligands and determine how they modulate protein function in cancer and immune-related systems. This is a temporary, mentored, one-year appointment with the possibility of renewal based on funding, performance, and university policy. The anticipated start date is Spring 2027. Work will be performed primarily on site at CWRU in Cleveland, Ohio. The position will not involve animal models or animal-derived samples. Supervisor/PI: Dr. Yongfeng Tao Responsibilities and Duties: Design and execute biochemical, molecular, chemical biology, chemical proteomics, and cell-based experiments addressing ligand discovery and protein function in cancer and immunology. Analyze, interpret, and document experimental data, including quantitative and statistical assessment, quality-control review, reproducibility checks, and preparation of figures, tables, and written summaries. Develop, optimize, troubleshoot, and validate assays and workflows, including cell culture, protein or biochemical assays, ligand-engagement assays, proteomics sample preparation, and functional cell-based assays. Prepare manuscripts, conference presentations, internal reports, and fellowship or grant applications, and pursue fellowship or grant funding as appropriate for career stage and project goals. Mentor and train undergraduate students, graduate students, and junior laboratory members in experimental planning, laboratory techniques, data organization, and safe, reproducible research practices. Maintain laboratory notebooks, protocols, data files, sample inventories, and research documentation; participate in lab meetings and postdoctoral training; and support routine laboratory operations. Occasional evening or weekend work may be required for time-sensitive experiments or deadlines. Minimum Qualifications: PhD or equivalent degree, completed by the appointment start date, in cell biology, molecular biology, cancer biology, immunology, biochemistry, chemical biology, or a related biomedical or chemical sciences field. Research experience obtained through doctoral training in a relevant field; no prior postdoctoral experience is required. Strong experimental judgment and ability to design, execute, troubleshoot, and interpret biochemical, molecular, chemical biology, chemical proteomics, or cell-based research workflows. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to prepare figures, summaries, manuscripts, presentations, and fellowship or grant materials. Strong organization, documentation, and data-management skills supporting reproducibility, compliance, and publication-quality research. Ability to work independently within an active mentored relationship and collaboratively with laboratory members, trainees, core facilities, and collaborators; ability to work on site and comply with laboratory safety and PPE requirements. Must be eligible to work in the United States by the appointment start date. Preferred Qualifications: Experience with one or more of the following: cell culture, molecular biology, protein biochemistry, chemical biology, chemical proteomics, cancer biology, immunology, or quantitative data analysis. Apply through Interfolio. The review of applications will begin five days after the job announcement is posted. Applicants should submit the following materials: Curriculum vitae, including a list of publications. A brief letter describing research interests, relevant experience, and fit with the Tao laboratory's research program. Proof of terminal degree, such as a diploma or transcript, or documentation that all degree requirements will be completed before the anticipated start date. Names and contact information for three professional references. Reference letters may be requested later and, if requested, should be submitted directly to the hiring mentor. Cleveland Pay Transparency Ordinance Statement: In compliance with the City of Cleveland’s Pay Transparency Ordinance (effective October 27,2025), the annual starting base salary range for this position is $55,000 - $63,480. CWRU considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Postdocs receive individual and family healthcare benefits, life insurance, and employee assistance. Case Western Reserve University is an equal opportunity employer. All applicants are protected under federal and state laws and university policy from discrimination based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, national or ethnic origin, protected veteran status, disability, age and genetic information. Case Western Reserve University complies with the Americans with Disabilities Act regarding reasonable accommodations for applicants with disabilities Applications requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the CWRU Office of Equity at 216.368.3066 to request a reasonable accommodation. Determinations as to granting reasonable accommodations for any applicant will be made on a case-by-case basis.",
      "department": "Pharmacology (PD)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/182628": {
      "title": "Health psychology postdoctoral fellowship",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-04-29 Location: 10524 Euclid Avenue, Cleveland, OH 44106 The Behavioral Health Institute and Heart and Vascular Institute at University Hospitals Cleveland Medical Center invites applications for our two-year Clinical Psychology Fellowship with a focus on clinical research in the area of health psychology. Train within a nationally recognized academic medical center affiliated with Case Western Research University School of Medicine where fellows will receive from day one with access to existing datasets for rapid publication, and work alongside faculty with NIH (NIMH, NHLBI), AHA, and industry funding. Fellows will spend 50% in research (participating in the development of a Behavioral Medicine Research program in collaboration with the renowned UH Hearth and Vasuclar Institute, analyzing existing  datasets from recenlty completed R01 studies, manuscript preparation, grant writing training, collaboration on active grant applications), 40% in clinical activities (individual/group behavioral interventions for cardiovascular patients, multidisciplinary clinics, outpatient clinics), and 10% in professional development (weekly supervision, reserch team meetings, teaching opportunities). Will be license eligible by the completinon of the 2-year fellowship. PhD graduates or anticipating graduation in 2026 (clinical psychology) from APA-accredited programs with completed/near completed internships and defended dissertation, demonstraitng strong interest in behavioral medicine, cardiovascular disease, and research career development. Competitive NIH-level stipend plus comprehense benefits and professional development funds. In compliance with the City of Cleveland’s Pay Transparency Ordinance (effective October 27, 2025), the annual starting base salary range for this position is $55,000 - $63,480. CWRU considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Postdocs receive individual and family healthcare benefits, life insurance, and employee assistance.",
      "department": "Psychiatry UH (PD)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/187896": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-22 Location: MetroHealth Research Institute  Supervisor/PI: Dr. Khalid Sossey-Alaoui 1.    Work in a project that deals with role of Kindlin-2 in driving the progression and metastasis of breast cancer with special emphasis of TNBC tumor interaction with immune microenvironment. 2.    Ability to work independently, including designing and executing experiments in consultation with PI; presenting the data in raw format as well as in high quality format during lab meetings as well as local, regional, national and international conferences. The candidate must have excellent scientific oral and writing skills to be able to put the data in publication-quality formats. 3.    Expertise in basic molecular biology techniques 4.    Expertise in bioinformatics 5.    Experience and/or interest in tumor biology and animal models 6.    A strong interest in translational cancer research 7.    Be able to work with other members of the lab and to mentor graduate students and research assistants. · Doctorate in Molecular Biology. · 1-5 years of experience with cancer biology, molecular signaling and bioinformatics. · Excellent written and oral communication skills necessary. · Must be eligible to work in the United States. \"In compliance with the City of Cleveland’s Pay Transparency Ordinance (effective October 27, 2025), the annual starting base salary range for this position is $55,000 - $63,480. CWRU considers factors such as (but not limited to) the specific grant funding and the terms of the research grant when extending an offer. Postdocs receive individual and family healthcare benefits, life insurance, and employee assistance.\"",
      "department": "MetroHealth Medical Center (PD)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/183491": {
      "title": "Student Operations Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-06-15 Location: Flora Stone Mather Center for Women Position Overview: In conjunction with the Operations Manager, the Mather Center Student Assistants will support the daily operations of the Flora Stone Mather Center for Women. In addition, Mather Center Student Assistants will support the planning, implementation, and publicity of programs and events associated with the Flora Stone Mather Center for Women. Responsibilities: Welcome guests, answer phone calls, and assist with questions Direct visitors to the lactation room and provide an orientation, if needed Restock snacks/coffee, giveaways, and other items at the front of the Mather Center Support the general maintenance and upkeep of the office and storage closets Keep track of inventory and keep inventory organized Photocopy and distribute events flyers and other marketing materials Label and distribute mass mailings Support the office staff in program preparation, implementation, evaluation, and other assigned tasks Manage on-site set-up and clean-up of Mather Center events Other minor duties as assigned Compensation $14.00 per hour Federal Work Study preferred Day and evening shifts Qualifications: Applicants must be enrolled as a full-time degree-seeking undergraduate student at CWRU Preferred Skills: Ability to use Google Workspace and interest in learning new software as necessary Excellent interpersonal, verbal, and written communication skills The ability to work both independently and in a team-oriented environment Must be self-motivated with excellent time, organizational, and project management skills The ability to work under pressure in a very busy environment Able to maintain confidentiality Desire to learn about social justice issues",
      "department": "Flora Stone Mather Center for Women (STUDENT)"
    }
  },
